{"name":"Hot Fever","hex":"#d40301","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Hote."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to heat and conditions which produce it.\n Having or giving off a high temperature.","Having or giving off a high temperature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hot"},{"term":"fever","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A higher than normal body temperature of a person (or, generally, a mammal), usually caused by disease.","Any of various diseases."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put into a fever; to affect with fever.","To become fevered."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fever"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fever","blocks":[{"text":"Fever or pyrexia in humans is a symptom of an anti-infection defense mechanism that appears with body temperature exceeding the normal range caused by an increase in the body's temperature set point in the hypothalamus. There is no single agreed-upon upper limit for normal temperature: sources use values ranging between 37.2 and 38.3 °C in humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Symptoms-fever.jpg/330px-Symptoms-fever.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fever","wikidata":"Q38933"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg/500px-Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg/1280px-Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg/500px-Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Febr